The {{nihongo|'''Nagata Line'''|長田線|Nagata-sen}} is a [[rapid transit]] line operated by [[Okami Metro Co., Ltd.]] in [[Okami]], [[Okaiken]]. It is designated as '''Line 7''' of the Okami Metro network and is distinguished by its pink color ({{color box|#ff0099|border=darkgray}}). At {{cvt|62.3|km|mi|abbr=on}} with 38 stations, it is the second-longest line in the Okami Metro network after the [[Tozai Line (Okami)|Tozai Line]] ({{cvt|64.2|km|mi|abbr=on}}, 64 stations) and one of the longest metro lines on the planet [[Adonia]]. The line runs from [[Toji-cho]] in the far north to [[Komorebi-machi]] in the south, traversing a diverse range of districts from the suburban outskirts of [[Karasaki]] through the urban core and into the southern districts of [[Shigenobu]].

The Nagata Line is notable for its historical use of [[linear motor]] technology. When it opened in 1990, the line was equipped with the [[Okami Metro 70 series|70 series]] linear motor trains, which were among the first linear motor trains deployed on the Okami Metro network. The linear motor technology was chosen to allow for smaller tunnel bore diameters, which was particularly important for the line's route through the dense, older districts of central Okami where deep-bore tunneling was required. In subsequent years, the line was upgraded to standard 8-car '''Type B''' trains, and the 70 series was retired.

The line serves 13 major interchange hubs, making it one of the most connected lines in the network. Key interchanges include connections to the {{OMSN|H|00}} [[Hitotsubashi Line]] at [[Hana-cho (H)|Hana-cho]] and [[Mokkou-machi (H)|Mokkou-machi]], the {{OMSN|I|00}} [[Imaichi Line]] and {{OMSN|D|00}} [[Tedorigawa Line]] at [[Amatsumara-machi (N)|Amatsumara-machi]], the {{OMSN|S|00}} [[Shitayanagi Line]] at [[Kashi (N)|Kashi]], [[Shou (S)|Shou]], and [[Byakudou-cho (S)|Byakudou-cho]], the {{OMSN|C|00}} [[Chūō Line (Okami)|Chūō Line]] at [[Shiokaze-machi (C)|Shiokaze-machi]] and [[Ajisai-machi (C)|Ajisai-machi]], the {{OMSN|B|00}} [[Nanboku Line (Okami)|Nanboku Line]] at [[Tokushige (B)|Tokushige]], the {{OMSN|M|00}} [[Miyoshi Line]] and {{OMSN|T|00}} [[Tozai Line (Okami)|Tozai Line]] at [[Rokumanji (M)|Rokumanji]], the {{OMSN|K|00}} [[Tokuyama Line]] at [[Kuninushi (K)|Kuninushi]], the {{OMSN|A|00}} [[Asahi Line]] and {{OMSN|G|00}} [[Tokushige Line]] at [[Takagawa (A)|Takagawa]], and the {{OMSN|I|00}} [[Imaichi Line]] at [[Minami-Sasame (N)|Minami-Sasame]].

==History==

===Planning and linear motor technology===
The Nagata Line was conceived in the early 1980s as part of the third major expansion phase of the Okami Municipal Subway. By this time, the existing six-line network was experiencing severe congestion, and the Municipal Transportation Bureau identified the need for a long-distance radial line that would connect the far northern and southern suburbs with the city center. The route was designed to pass through some of the oldest and most densely built-up districts of Okami, including the historic [[Nagata]] ward, from which the line takes its name.

The decision to use [[linear motor]] technology was driven by the geological and urban constraints of the route. The line needed to pass beneath dense historic districts where deep-bore tunneling was required, and the smaller tunnel bore diameter made possible by linear motor trains — which have a lower profile than conventional rotary-motor trains — significantly reduced construction costs and disruption to surface structures. The [[Okami Metro 70 series|70 series]] linear motor trains were developed specifically for this line by [[Okami Heavy Industries]], marking the first deployment of linear motor technology on the Okami Metro network.

===Opening and early operations===
The Nagata Line opened on {{date|1990|df=y}}, initially operating between [[Toji-cho]] and [[Rokumanji (N)|Rokumanji]] stations. The opening was a significant milestone for the Okami Municipal Subway, as it introduced linear motor technology to the network and provided the first direct rapid transit connection between the northern suburbs of [[Karasaki]] and the central city. The 70 series trains operated in 6-car formations, reflecting the smaller platform dimensions associated with linear motor infrastructure.

The southern extension from Rokumanji to [[Komorebi-machi]] was completed in stages over the following decade, with the full 38-station, {{cvt|62.3|km|mi|abbr=on}} line reaching its current configuration in the early 2000s. This extension brought the line through the southern wards of [[Minami]], [[Tama]], [[Chiyoda]], [[Nagata]], [[Habu]], and [[Shigenobu]], serving both densely populated urban neighborhoods and developing suburban areas.

===Upgrading to Type B and expansion===
By the late 2000s, the limitations of the linear motor 70 series trains had become apparent. The smaller car size constrained passenger capacity, and the linear motor technology, while innovative, resulted in higher maintenance costs compared to conventional rotary-motor trains. As passenger demand on the Nagata Line continued to grow, the Municipal Transportation Bureau made the decision to upgrade the line to standard 8-car '''Type B''' trains.

The upgrade required extensive platform modifications at all 38 stations, including platform extensions and height adjustments to accommodate the larger Type B trains. The overhead catenary system ({{1,500 V DC}}) was retained, as it had been installed for the linear motor trains and was compatible with the new rolling stock. The [[Okami Metro 70 series|70 series]] was gradually retired and replaced by the [[Okami Metro 5000 series|5000 series]] Type B trains, manufactured by [[Okami Heavy Industries]]. The 5000 series Type B trains are 8-car formations with a total length of approximately {{cvt|160|m|ft|abbr=on}} and a car width of {{cvt|2.8|m|ft|abbr=on}}.

In 2023, the flagship [[Okami Metro 4000 series|4000 series]] trains were also introduced on the Nagata Line as part of the system-wide modernization program. The 4000 series features a distinctive futuristic design with hexagonal windows and onboard Wi-Fi integrated with the city's Satori Network.

The line was also expanded with the addition of new stations at [[Kashi (N)|Kashi]] and [[Higashi-Yamada]], serving growing residential developments in the [[Sugamo]] and [[Higashi]] wards respectively. These additions brought the total station count to its current 38.

