==Rolling stock==

The Nagata Line currently operates two types of rolling stock, both classified as '''Type B''' (8-car, {{cvt|160|m|ft|abbr=on}}, {{cvt|2.8|m|ft|abbr=on}} wide):

* '''[[Okami Metro 5000 series|5000 series]]''': Introduced in 2018 as part of the system-wide fleet replacement following privatization. Manufactured by [[Okami Heavy Industries]], the 5000 series Type B trains replaced the legacy linear motor 70 series and provided significantly increased passenger capacity. The trains feature energy-efficient propulsion, regenerative braking, and the Satori-AI integrated passenger management system.
* '''[[Okami Metro 4000 series|4000 series]]''': Introduced in 2023 as the flagship fleet of the Okami Metro network. The 4000 series features a distinctive futuristic design with hexagonal windows and onboard Wi-Fi integrated with the city's Satori Network. These trains were specifically commissioned to serve as the face of the transport network.

===Historical rolling stock===

* '''[[Okami Metro 70 series|70 series]]''': The original linear motor train used on the Nagata Line from its opening in 1990. The 70 series was developed by [[Okami Heavy Industries]] as one of the first linear motor trains deployed on the Okami Metro network. The trains operated in shorter formations and had a lower profile than conventional trains to accommodate the smaller tunnel bore diameter. The 70 series was retired following the line's upgrade to Type B trains.
* '''[[Okami Metro 3000 series|3000 series]]''': A legacy linear motor train that also operated on the Nagata Line. The 3000 series is being retired as the line completes its transition to standard Type B rolling stock.


